greater the busco score the more complete capture your genome has 

repeat landscape plots look cool

GATK is gold standard snp calling 

breaker 3 pipeline good for genome annoation  

repeats hinder completeness as cant know true order of a bunch of similar short reads therefore long reads are a way to overcome this 

link for genome assembly code https://otagobioinformaticsspringschool.github.io/long-read-assembly/pages/2_familiarise_w_data/



#cleaning data using cut adapts which cuts addapters was for pacbio data 
fast qc can also help you identify the adapter 

#for nanopore you dont remove adaptors but you only want to keep long reads 
module load SeqKit/2.4.0
seqkit seq \
    -m 50000 \
    ont_ul_5k_reads.fq.gz \
    | pigz > ont_ul_5k_reads.50kb.fq.gz &

#this code checks if it purged the short reads
NanoComp --fastq \
    ont_ul_5k_reads.fq.gz \
    ont_ul_5k_reads.50kb.fq.gz \
    --names ONT_UL ONT_UL_50kb \
    --outdir nanocomp_ul_vs_ul_50kb

#then check you didnt remove too many reads and how much data you lost to see if it make sense do this by checking the report 

#phasing notes 
#need to know which base goes from paternal or maternal 
#high c also allows you to infer structure of genome 

#kmers are just differnent read frames kmers are actually the key part of genome assembly 
kmer read code 
meryl count \
    compress \
    k=30 \
    threads=2 \
    memory=8 \
    HG003_HiSeq30x_05M_reads_R1.fastq.gz \
    output paternal_05M_compress.k30.meryl



